INFO     rag_service.services.generation_service:generation_service.py:427 {"event": "rag_query_trace", "query": "What about corticosteroids?", "retrieval_query": null, "system_prompt_version": "440b2406", "retrieved_chunks": [{"title": "Protocol Ulcerative Colitis", "page": 23, "section": "1.3 Benefit / Risk Aspects", "score": null, "scores": [0.6954414, 0.6954414, 0.1482937, 0.1482937], "had_context": true}, {"title": "Protocol Ulcerative Colitis", "page": 37, "section": "4.3.3 Other Restrictions", "score": null, "scores": [0.02964751, 0.02964751], "had_context": true}], "context_char_count": 4306, "response": "Source: Protocol Ulcerative Colitis\n\nCorticosteroids are used orally and systemically to induce remission in patients who do not respond to 5-ASA, but they are undesirable for maintenance treatment because of frequent and sometimes serious adverse effects, including hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal-axis suppression, hyperglycaemia/insulin resistance, cataracts, and osteoporosis.\n[Section 1.3 Benefit / Risk Aspects \u00b7 p.23]\n\nDuring the trial, patients receiving stable-dose concomitant UC treatment at Visit 2 must remain on a stable dose; corticosteroids are the exception, and tapering is permitted at the Investigator\u2019s discretion.\n[Section 4.3.3 Other Restrictions \u00b7 p.37]", "sources": [{"name": "Protocol Ulcerative Colitis", "page": 23, "section": "1.3 Benefit / Risk Aspects", "exactText": "Oral, systemic corticosteroids are used for induction of remission in patients not responding to 5-ASA. Corticosteroids have frequent and occasionally serious side effects, e.g. hypothalamicpituitary-adrenal (HPA)-axis suppression, hyperglycaemia/insulin resistance, cataracts, and osteoporosis (notably, IBD itself is a risk factor for osteopenia/osteoporosis, with Crohn's patients being most affected), and systemic corticosteroids are therefore undesirable in maintenance treatment.", "bboxes": [[71.99999999999972, 338.18398291015615, 552.8399999999998, 1